 125: \sectionbanner{LAUNCH --- Complex Zeros + Modeling (teacher models on screen)}
 126: 
 127: \begin{callout}{\IconBook\ COMPLEX-ZERO RULES (keep printed on your page)}{calloutgreen}
 128: \textbullet\ A polynomial with real coefficients that has complex zeros has them in CONJUGATE PAIRS.\\
 129: \textbullet\ If \(a + bi\) is a zero, then \(a - bi\) must also be a zero.\\
 130: \textbullet\ A degree-\(n\) polynomial has exactly \(n\) zeros (counting multiplicity). Some may be real, some complex.\\
 131: \textbullet\ Example: if a cubic has a real zero at \(x = 4\) and a complex zero \(2 + 3i\), its third zero must be \(2 - 3i\).
 132: \end{callout}
 133: 
 134: \begin{callout}{\IconRuler\ MODELING RULES (for the Storage Box)}{calloutyellow}
 135: \textbullet\ Set up a factored-form volume function \(V(x) = (\text{expr}_1)(\text{expr}_2)(\text{expr}_3)\).\\
 136: \textbullet\ Each factor is a physical dimension --- label each: length, width, height.\\
 137: \textbullet\ Use the physical constraint (e.g., ``length is greatest'') to decide which factor is which dimension.\\
 138: \textbullet\ To find dimensions at \(V = 10\) ft\(^3\), solve the cubic equation --- show all work.
 139: \end{callout}
