 252: \begin{callout}{\IconBook\ COMPLEX-ZERO RULES (keep printed on your page)}{calloutgreen}
 253: \textbullet\ A polynomial with real coefficients that has complex zeros has them in CONJUGATE PAIRS.\\
 254: \textbullet\ If \(a + bi\) is a zero, then \(a - bi\) must also be a zero.\\
 255: \textbullet\ A degree-\(n\) polynomial has exactly \(n\) zeros (counting multiplicity). Some may be real, some complex.\\
 256: \textbullet\ Example: if a cubic has a real zero at \(x=4\) and a complex zero \(2 + 3i\), its third zero must be \(2 - 3i\).
 257: \end{callout}
 258: 
 259: \begin{callout}{\IconWarn\ MODELING RULES (for the Storage Box)}{calloutyellow}
 260: \textbullet\ Set up a factored-form volume function \(V(x) = (\mathrm{expr}_1)(\mathrm{expr}_2)(\mathrm{expr}_3)\).\\
 261: \textbullet\ Each factor is a physical dimension --- label each: length, width, height.\\
 262: \textbullet\ Use the physical constraint (e.g., ``length is greatest'') to decide which factor is which dimension.\\
 263: \textbullet\ To find dimensions at \(V = 10\ \text{ft}^3\), solve the cubic equation --- show all work.
 264: \end{callout}
