: \"4-1\", \"source\": \"Savvas Try It 3 (lesson 4-1 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d matches Example 3)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": \"questionbank/images/4-1_savvas_example3_tryit3_question.png\", \"has_visual\": true, \"prompt\": \"Try It 3. The amount of time it takes for an ice cube to melt varies inversely with the air temperature in degrees Celsius. At 20\u00b0C, the ice will melt in 20 minutes. How long will it take the ice to melt if the temperature is 30\u00b0C?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Guided practice matched to Example 3 application pattern. Student must translate contextual inverse-variation statement into equation form, find k, then substitute.\", \"topics\": [\"inverse-variation\", \"modeling\", \"application\"], \"tags\": [\"lesson-4-1\", \"try-it\", \"try-it-3\", \"savvas-practice\", \"application\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from TE): \u00e2\u2030\u02c613.3 minutes. Reasoning: k = 20\u00c2\u00b720 = 400; t = 400/30 \u00e2\u2030\u02c6 13.3.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": true, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"role\": \"explore-tps\", \"standards\": [\"A-CED.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [\"4-1-savvas-try-it-2-lesson-4-1-matches-examp\"], \"rehearses\": [\"4-1-savvas-q17\", \"4-1-savvas-q23\"], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"model-inverse-variation-context\", \"solve-for-k\", \"evaluate-inverse-variation\"]}\n130:{\"id\": \"4-1-savvas-q12\", \"lesson\": \"4-1\", \"source\": \"Savvas Practice #12 (Higher Order Thinking, lesson 4-1, anchors Example 3)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": \"questionbank/images/4-1_savvas_q12_question.png\", \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"12. Higher Order Thinking: Suppose y varies inversely as the square of x. If x is multiplied by 4, what is true for the value of y?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Savvas-declared DOK-2 item anchored to Example 3. Introduces inverse-square variation (y = k/x^2). Student must reason that if x becomes 4x, then new y = k/(4x)^2 = k/(16x^2) = (1/16) * original y. Multi-step algebraic reasoning with a twist (square).\", \"topics\": [\"inverse-variation\", \"inverse-square\", \"constant-of-variation\", \"proportional-reasoning\"], \"tags\": [\"lesson-4-1\", \"savvas-practice\", \"example-3-anchor\", \"higher-order-thinking\", \"inverse-square\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from TE): Divide by 16. Reasoning: y = k/x^2. When x -> 4x, new y = k/(4x)^2 = k/(16x^2) = (1/16)y_original.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-CED.A.2\"], \"prereq_ids\": [\"4-1-savvas-try-it-3-lesson-4-1-matches-examp\"], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"recognize-inverse-square-variation\", \"generalize-numerical-result\"]}\n131:{\"id\": \"4-1-savvas-q17\", \"lesson\": \"4-1\", \"source\": \"Savvas Practice #17 (lesson 4-1, See Example 3)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": \"questionbank/images/4-1_savvas_q17_question.png\", \"has_visual\": true, \"visual_type\": \"graph\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"17. The wavelength, w, of a radio wave varies inversely to its frequency, f, as shown in the graph. The graph shows w (m) on the y-axis from 0 to 1200, and f (kilohertz) on the x-axis from 0 to 10000, with the curve w = k/f passing through data points including (1000, 300), (2000, 150), (4000, 75), (6000, 50), (8000, 37.5), (10000, 30). A radio wave with a frequency of 1,000 kilohertz has a length of 300 m. What is the frequency when the wavelength is 375 m?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Savvas-declared DOK-2 item anchored to Example 3. Contextual inverse variation with graph reading. Student must extract k = wf = 1000 * 300 = 300,000, then solve f = 300,000/375 = 800 kHz.\", \"topics\": [\"inverse-variation\", \"modeling\", \"application\", \"graph-reading\", \"constant-of-variation\"], \"tags\": [\"lesson-4-1\", \"savvas-practice\", \"example-3-anchor\", \"modeling\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from TE): 800 kHz. Transcription and graph description verified against Gemini 3.1 Pro (LaTeX output with w = 300000/f curve and data points).\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-CED.A.2\"], \"prereq_ids\": [\"4-1-savvas-try-it-3-lesson-4-1-matches-examp\"], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [\"6-3-savvas-q54\"], \"skill_tokens\": [\"model-inverse-variation-context\", \"read-graph-values\"]}\n132:{\"id\": \"4-1-savvas-q23\", \"lesson\": \"4-1\", \"source\": \"Savvas Practice #23 (Model With Mathematics, lesson 4-1, anchors Example 3)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": \"questionbank/images/4-1_savvas_q23_question.png\", \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"23. Model With Mathematics: Boyle's Law states that the pressure exerted by fixed quantity of a gas, P, varies inversely with the volume the gas occupies, V, assuming constant temperature. The volume and air pressure of a volleyball are 300 in^3 and 4.5 psi. The volume and air pressure of a basketball are 415 in^3 and 8 psi. How much smaller would the volleyball have to be to be equal the air pressure of the basketball?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Savvas-declared DOK-2 item anchored to Example 3. Multi-step physics-contextualized inverse variation. Student finds volleyball's k = PV = 300 * 4.5 = 1350, then finds new volume at P = 8 psi: V = 1350/8 = 168.75 in^3. Reduction: 300 - 168.75 = 131.25 in^3.\", \"topics\": [\"inverse-variation\", \"modeling\", \"application\", \"physics\", \"boyles-law\"], \"tags\": [\"lesson-4-1\", \"savvas-practice\", \"example-3-anchor\", \"model-with-mathematics\"