5,1) (-5,-1) (-2,-4) (-1,-5)\n  83: };\n  84: \\node[text=warmred, anchor=west, font=\\small\\bfseries] at (axis cs:1.18,5.15) {\\((1,5)\\)};\n  85: \\node[text=warmred, anchor=west, font=\\small\\bfseries] at (axis cs:2.22,4.15) {\\((2,4)\\)};\n  86: \\node[text=warmred, anchor=west, font=\\small\\bfseries] at (axis cs:4.18,1.32) {\\((5,1)\\)};\n  87: \\node[text=warmred, anchor=east, font=\\small\\bfseries] at (axis cs:-4.85,-1.15) {\\((-5,-1)\\)};\n  88: \\node[text=warmred, anchor=east, font=\\small\\bfseries] at (axis cs:-1.78,-3.8) {\\((-2,-4)\\)};\n  89: \\node[text=warmred, anchor=east, font=\\small\\bfseries] at (axis cs:-0.78,-5.1) {\\((-1,-5)\\)};\n  90: \\node[text=warmred, font=\\Large\\bfseries] at (axis cs:4.35,-4.2) {X};\n  91: \\end{axis}\n  92: \\end{tikzpicture}\n  93: \\end{center}\n  94: }\n  95: \n  96: \\begin{document}\n  97: \n  98: \\packetheading{Lesson 4-1 \\textperiodcentered\\ Period 3 \\textperiodcentered\\ Student Packet}{Reciprocal Function + Translations --- Assessment-Critical}\n  99: \n 100: \\sectionbanner{OBJECTIVES}\n 101: {\\small\n 102: \\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1.25}\n 103: \\noindent\\begin{tabular}{|p{1.8in}|p{4.8in}|}\n 104: \\hline\n 105: \\textbf{Math Objective} & Graph the reciprocal function \\(f(x) = 1/x\\) and its translations \\(g(x) = 1/(x-h) + k\\). Identify vertical and horizontal asymptotes, state domain and range, and find intercepts where they exist. \\\\ \\hline\n 106: \\textbf{Language Objective} & Students will use the frame ``This graph is the parent \\(f(x) = 1/x\\) translated \\_\\_\\_ units \\_\\_\\_. The asymptotes are at \\(x =\\) \\_\\_\\_ and \\(y =\\) \\_\\_\\_.'' to describe any translated reciprocal function. \\\\ \\hline\n 107: \\textbf{Essential Understanding} & The reciprocal function has a vertical asymptote at \\(x = 0\\) and horizontal asymptote at \\(y = 0\\). Translations shift the asymptotes: \\(g(x) = 1/(x-h) + k\\) has asymptotes at \\(x = h\\) and \\(y = k\\). The point \\((h, k)\\) is the new ``center.'' \\\\ \\hline\n 108: \\end{tabular}\n 109: }\n 110: \n 111: \\sectionbanner{TOPIC GOALS / ESSENTIAL QUESTION / MATERIALS}\n 112: {\\small\n 113: \\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1.25}\n 114: \\noindent\\begin{tabular}{|p{1.8in}|p{4.8in}|}\n 115: \\hline\n 116: \\textbf{Topic Goals} & Students will identify asymptotes and describe translations for 6 reciprocal-function items. This is Topic 4 assessment-prep material. \\\\ \\hline\n 117: \\textbf{Essential Question} & How do the values of \\(h\\) and \\(k\\) in \\(g(x) = 1/(x-h) + k\\) determine the graph's asymptotes, domain, range, and intercepts? \\\\ \\hline\n 118: \\textbf{Materials} & Pencils; student packet; graphing calculator (optional, for verification). \\\\ \\hline\n 119: \\end{tabular}\n 120: }\n 121: \n 122: \\sectionbanner{LAUNCH --- Reciprocal Function + Translations (teacher models Ex 4 + Ex 5)}\n 123: \n 124: \\begin{callout}{\\IconBook\\ RECIPROCAL FUNCTION --- PARENT \\(f(x) = 1/x\\)}{calloutgreen}\n 125: \\textbullet\\ Vertical asymptote: \\(x = 0\\)\\\\\n 126: \\textbullet\\ Horizontal asymptote: \\(y = 0\\)\\\\\n 127: \\textbullet\\ Domain: \\(\\{x \\mid x \\neq 0\\}\\) \\hspace{1.8em} \\textbullet\\ Range: \\(\\{y \\mid y \\neq 0\\}\\)\\\\\n 128: \\textbullet\\ Graph: hyperbola in Quadrants I and III (two branches, no intersection)\n 129: \\end{callout}\n 130: \n 131: \\begin{callout}{\\IconBook\\ TRANSLATED RECIPROCAL --- \\(g(x) = 1/(x-h) + k\\)}{calloutyellow}\n 132: \\textbullet\\ Vertical asymptote shifts to: \\(x = h\\)\\\\\n 133: \\textbullet\\ Horizontal asymptote shifts to: \\(y = k\\)\\\\\n 134: \\textbullet\\ Domain: \\(\\{x \\mid x \\neq h\\}\\) \\hspace{1.8em} \\textbullet\\ Range: \\(\\{y \\mid y \\neq k\\}\\)\\\\\n 135: \\textbullet\\ The point \\((h, k)\\) is the ``new center'' --- where the asymptotes cross.\\\\\n 136: \\textbullet\\ Example: \\(g(x) = 1/(x-3) + 2\\) \\(\\rightarrow\\) asymptotes \\(x = 3\\) and \\(y = 2\\).\n 137: \n 138: \\medskip\n 139: To find INTERCEPTS:\\\\\n 140: \\hspace*{1.5em}\\textbullet\\ y-intercept: set \\(x = 0\\). \\(y = 1/(0-h) + k = -1/h + k.\\)\\\\\n 141: \\hspace*{1.5em}\\textbullet\\ x-intercept: set \\(y = 0\\). Solve \\(0 = 1/(x-h) + k \\rightarrow x = h - 1/k.\\)\n 142: \\end{callout}\n 143: \n 144: \\sectionbanner{EXPLORE --- Think-Pair-Share (35 min)}\n 145: \\textit{Work with a partner. Practice \\#19 is assessment-critical (asymptotes + intercepts) --- plan \\(\\sim\\)8 min for it.}\n 146: \n 147: \\textbf{Bank-item labels (in order):}\n 148: \\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em,itemsep=2pt,topsep=2pt]\n 149:   \\item Try It 4 --- Graph \\(g(x) = 10/x\\) (scaled parent)\n 150:   \\item Practice \\#11 --- Error Analysis: \\(y = 5/x\\)\n 151:   \\item Practice \\#18 --- Graph \\(y = -2/x\\) (reflected parent)\n 152:   \\item Try It 5 --- Graph \\(g(x) = 1/(x+2) - 4\\) (translated)\n 153:   \\item Practice \\#19 \\IconStar\\ ASSESSMENT CRITICAL --- \\(g(x) = 1/(x-2) + 6\\) + intercepts\n 154:   \\item Practice \\#21 --- Video game storage (modeled)\n 155: \\end{enumerate}\n 156: \n 157: \\bankitem{Try It 4 --- Graph \\(g(x) = 10/x\\) (scaled parent)}{%\n 158: Try It 4. Graph the function \\(g(x) = 10/x\\). What are the domain, range, and asymptotes of the function?\n 159: \\reciprocalblankgrid\n 160: }{}\n 161: \n 162: \\bankitem{Practice \\#11 --- Error Analysis: \\(y = 5/x\\)}{%\n 163: 11. Error Analysis: Describe and correct the error a student made in graphing the function \\(y = 5/x\\). The student's graph shows labeled points \\((1, 5)\\), \\((2, 4)\\), \\((5, 1)\\) in Quadrant I and \\((-5, -1)\\), \\((-2, -4)\\), \\((-1, -5)\\) in Quadrant III, with a large red X indicating the graph is incorrect.\n 164: \\qelevengraph\n 165: \\vspace{0.25in}\n 166: }{}\n 167: \n 168: \\bankitem{Practice \\#18 --- Graph \\(y = -2/x\\) (reflected parent)}{%\n 169: 18. Graph the function \\(y = -2/x\\). What are the domain, range, and asymptotes of the function?\n 170: \\reciprocalblankgrid\n 171: }{}\n 172: \n 173: \\bankitem{Try It 5 --- Graph \\(g(x) = 1/(x+2) - 4\\) (translated)}{%\n 174: Try It 5. Graph \\(g(x) = 1/(x + 2) - 4\\). What are the equations of the asymptotes? What are the domain and range?\n 175: \\reciprocalblankgrid\n 176: }{}\n 177: \n 178: \\bankitem{Practice \\#19 \\IconStar\\ ASSESSMENT CRITICAL --- \\(g(x) = 1/(x-2) + 6\\) + intercepts}{%\n 179: 19. Graph \\(g(x) = 1/(x - 2) + 6\\). What are the equations of the asymptotes? What are the domain and range? What are the intercepts?\n 180: \\reciprocalblankgrid\n 181: }{}\n 182: \n 183: \\bankitem{Practice \\#21 --- Video game storage (modeled)}{%\n 184: 21. Use Structure: The number of downloaded games that can be stored on a video game system varies inversely with the average size of a video game. A certain video game system can store 160 games when the average size of a game is 2.0 gigabytes (GB).\\\\\n 185: \n 186: \\textbf{(a)} Write an inverse equation that relates the number of games \\(n\\) that will fit on the video game system as a function of the average game size \\(s\\) in GB.\\\\\n 187: \n 188: \\textbf{(b)} Use the inverse relationship to complete the table of values.\n 189: \\begin{center}\n 190: \\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1.35}\n 191: \\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|c|c|}\n 192: \\hline\n 193: \\textbf{Game Size (GB), \\(s\\)} & 1.0 & 2.5 & 3.0 & 4.0 \\\\ \\hline\n 194: \\textbf{Number of Games, \\(n\\)} & \\entrybox & \\entrybox & \\entrybox & \\entrybox \\\\ \\hline\n 195: \\end{tabular}\n 196: \\end{center}\n 197: \n 198: \\textbf{(c)} Sketch a graph of this inverse relationship on a coordinate plane.\n 199: \\gamesblankgrid\n 200: }{}\n 201: \n 202: \\sectionbanner{SHARE / SUMMARY (5 min)}\n 203: \n 204: \\begin{sentenceframebox}\n 205: \\textbf{Sentence frames}\n 206: \n 207: Pair share (\\#19): ``This graph is the parent \\(f(x) = 1/x\\) translated \\_\\_\\_ units \\_\\_\\_ and \\_\\_\\_ units \\_\\_\\_. The asymptotes are \\(x =\\) \\_\\_\\_ and \\(y =\\) \\_\\_\\_. The intercepts are \\_\\_\\_.''\n 208: \n 209: Whole class: one pair reads \\#19. Class signals thumbs up/sideways.\n 210: \\end{sentenceframebox}\n 211: \n 212: \\begin{callout}{\\IconChart\\ CONCEPT SUMMARY (your reference for Topic 4 assessment)}{calloutell}\n 213: INVERSE VARIATION (\\(y = k/x\\)):\\\\\n 214: \\hspace*{1.5em}--- WORDS: As one variable increases, the other decreases; their product \\(k\\) is constant.\\\\\n 215: \\hspace*{1.5em}--- ALGEBRA: \\(y = k/x\\) where \\(k \\neq 0\\)\\\\\n 216: \\hspace*{1.5em}--- GRAPH: parent hyperbola, asymptotes \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y = 0\\)\n 217: \n 218: \\medskip\n 219: RECIPROCAL FUNCTION TRANSLATIONS (\\(y = a/(x-h) + k\\)):\\\\\n 220: \\hspace*{1.5em}--- WORDS: The reciprocal function can shift left/right (\\(h\\)) and up/down (\\(k\\)).\\\\\n 221: \\hspace*{1.5em}--- ALGEBRA: \\(y = a/(x-h) + k\\)\\\\\n 222: \\hspace*{1.5em}--- GRAPH: asymptotes shift to \\(x = h\\) and \\(y = k\\); new ``center'' at \\((h, k)\\).\n 223: \\end{callout}\n 224: \n 225: \\sectionbanner{EXIT}\n 226: \n 227: \\begin{summaryexitbox}{EXIT TICKET --- Pre-Assessment Confidence Check}\n 228: I can identify asymptotes of \\(y = 1/(x-h) + k\\). \\hfill Confidence: 1 / 2 / 3 / 4 / 5\n 229: \n 230: I can describe a translation from \\(f(x) = 1/x\\) to \\(g(x)\\). \\hfill Confidence: 1 / 2 / 3 / 4 / 5\n 231: \n 232: One thing I want to review before Topic 4 assessment: \\dotfill\n 233: \\end{summaryexitbox}\n 234: \n 235: \\clearpage\n 236: \n 237: \\sectionbanner{OPTIONAL REINFORCEMENT (not collected)}\n 238: \\textit{Extra stretch for fast finishers. Try without a calculator first.}\n 239: \n 240: \\bankitem{Optional \\#1  (Savvas SAT/ACT style)}{%\n 241: 25. SAT/ACT. In a given set of experiments, \\(t\\) and \\(w\\) always vary inversely. When \\(t = 0.4\\) seconds, \\(w = 172.8\\) grams. How much time has passed when \\(w = 14.4\\) grams?\n 242: }{%\n 243:   \\answerchoice{A}{2.4 s}\n 244:   \\answerchoice{B}{4.8 s}\n 245:   \\answerchoice{C}{27.648 s}\n 246:   \\answerchoice{D}{995.328 s}\n 247: }\n 248: \n 249: \\end{document}\n===== tex/L41_P3_teacher.tex =====\n   1: \\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n   2: \\usepackage{preamble}\n   3: \n   4: \\newcommand{\\IconCheck}{[CHECK]}\n   5: \\newcommand{\\IconMic}{[MIC]}\n   6: \\newcommand{\\IconClipboard}{[CLIPBOARD]}\n   7: \\newcommand{\\IconChart}{[CHART]}\n   8: \n   9: \\newcommand{\\phasetag}[1]{%\n  10:   {\\small\\itshape Tag: #1\\par}\n  11: }\n  12: \n  13: \\newcommand{\\phaseitems}[1]{%\n  14:   \\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em,itemsep=2pt,topsep=2pt]\n  15:   #1\n  16:   \\end{itemize}%\n  17: }\n  18: \n  19: \\newcommand{\\teacheranswerbox}[2]{%\n  20:   \\begin{callout}{#1}{calloutgreen}\n  21:   #2\n  22:   \\end{callout}\n  23: }\n  24: \n  25: \\newcommand{\\entrybox}{\\framebox[1.2em]{\\rule{0pt}{0.95em}}}\n  26: \n  27: \\newcommand{\\reciprocalblankgrid}{%\n  28: \\begin{center}\n  29: \\begin{tikzpicture}\n  30: \\begin{axis}[\n  31:   width=0.78\\linewidth,\n  32:   height=2.15in,\n  33:   xmin=-10, xmax=10,\n  34:   ymin=-10, ymax=10,\n  35:   axis lines=middle,\n  36:   grid=both,\n  37:   minor tick num=4,\n  38:   xtick={-10,-5,0,5,10},\n  39:   ytick={-10,-5,0,5,10},\n  40:   tick label style={font=\\small},\n  41:   label style={font=\\small},\n  42:   major grid style={draw=black!25},\n  43:   minor grid style={draw=black!10},\n  44:   xlabel={$x$},\n  45:   ylabel={$y$},\n  46:   every axis x label/.style={at={(ticklabel* cs:1)},anchor=west},\n  47:   every axis y label/.style={at={(ticklabel* cs:1)},anchor=south}\n  48: ]\n  49: \\end{axis}\n  50: \\end{tikzpicture}\n  51: \\end{center}\n  52: }\n  53: \n  54: \\newcommand{\\gamesblankgrid}{%\n  55: \\begin{center}\n  56: \\begin{tikzpicture}\n  57: \\begin{axis}[\n  58:   width=0.78\\linewidth,\n  59:   height=2.2in,\n  60:   xmin=0, xmax=4.5,\n  61:   ymin=0, ymax=350,\n  62:   axis lines=left,\n  63:   grid=both,\n  64:   minor tick num=1,\n  65:   xtick={0,1,2,3,4},\n  66:   ytick={0,50,100,150,200,250,300,350},\n  67:   tick label style={font=\\small},\n  68:   label style={font=\\small},\n  69:   major grid style={draw=black!25},\n  70:   minor grid style={draw=black!10},\n  71:   xlabel={Game Size (GB), $s$},\n  72:   ylabel={Number of Games, $n$}\n  73: ]\n  74: \\end{axis}\n  75: \\end{tikzpicture}\n  76: \\end{center}\n  77: }\n  78: \n  79: \\newcommand{\\qelevengraph}{%\n  80: \\begin{center}\n  81: \\begin{tikzpicture}\n  82: \\begin{axis}[\n  83:   width=0.74\\linewidth,\n  84:   height=2.65in,\n  85:   xmin=-6, xmax=6,\n  86:   ymin=-6, ymax=6,\n  87:   axis lines=middle,\n  88:   grid=both,\n  89:   minor tick num=1,\n  90:   xtick={-4,-2,0,2,4},\n  91:   ytick={-4,-2,0,2,4},\n  92:   tick label style={font=\\small},\n  93:   label style={font=\\small},\n  94:   major grid style={draw=black!25},\n  95:   minor grid style={draw=black!10},\n  96:   clip=false,\n  97:   xlabel={$x$},\n  98:   ylabel={$y$},\n  99:   every axis x label/.style={at={(ticklabel* cs:1)},anchor=west},\n 100:   every axis y label/.style={at={(ticklabel* cs:1)},anchor=south}\n 101: ]\n 102: \\addplot[warmred, thick, smooth] coordinates {(0.65,6) (1,5) (2,4) (5,1) (5.8,0.7)};\n 103: \\addplot[warmred, thick, smooth] coordinates {(-5.8,-0.7) (-5,-1) (-2,-4) (-1,-5) (-0.65,-6)};\n 104: \\addplot[only marks, mark=*, mark size=2.6pt, color=warmred] coordinates {\n 105:   (1,5) (2,4) (5,1) (-5,-1) (-2,-4) (-1,-5)\n 106: };\n 107: \\node[text=warmred, anchor=west, font=\\small\\bfseries] at (axis cs:1.18,5.15) {\\((1,5)\\)};\n 108: \\node[text=warmred, anchor=west, font=\\small\\bfseries] at (axis cs:2.22,4.15) {\\((2,4)\\)};\n 109: \\node[text=warmred, anchor=west, font=\\small\\bfseries] at (axis cs:4.18,1.32) {\\((5,1)\\)};\n 110: \\node[text=warmred, anchor=east, font=\\small\\bfseries] at (axis cs:-4.85,-1.15) {\\((-5,-1)\\)};\n 111: \\node[text=warmred, anchor=east, font=\\small\\bfseries] at (axis cs:-1.78,-3.8) {\\((-2,-4)\\)};\n 112: \\node[text=warmred, anchor=east, font=\\small\\bfseries] at (axis cs:-0.78,-5.1) {\\((-1,-5)\\)};\n 113: \\node[text=warmred, font=\\Large\\bfseries] at (axis cs:4.35,-4.2) {X};\n 114: \\end{axis}\n 115: \\end{tikzpicture}\n 116: \\end{center}\n 117: }\n 118: \n 119: \\begin{document}\n 120: \n 121: \\packetheading{Lesson 4-1 \\textperiodcentered\\ Period 3 \\textperiodcentered\\ Teacher Edition}{Reciprocal Function + Translations --- Assessment-Critical}\n 122: \n 123: \\sectionbanner{OBJECTIVES}\n 124: {\\small\n 125: \\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1.25}\n 126: \\noindent\\begin{tabular}{|p{1.8in}|p{4.8in}|}\n 127: \\hline\n 128: \\textbf{Math Objective} & Graph the reciprocal function \\(f(x) = 1/x\\) and its translations \\(g(x) = 1/(x-h) + k\\). Identify vertical and horizontal asymptotes, state domain and range, and find intercepts where they exist. \\\\ \\hline\n 129: \\textbf{Language Objective} & Students will use the frame ``This graph is the parent \\(f(x) = 1/x\\) translated \\_\\_\\_ units \\_\\_\\_. The asymptotes are at \\(x =\\) \\_\\_\\_ and \\(y =\\) \\_\\_\\_.'' to describe any translated reciprocal function. \\\\ \\hline\n 130: \\textbf{Essential Understanding} & The reciprocal function has a vertical asymptote at \\(x = 0\\) and horizontal asymptote at \\(y = 0\\). Translations shift the asymptotes: \\(g(x) = 1/(x-h) + k\\) has asymptotes at \\(x = h\\) and \\(y = k\\). The point \\((h, k)\\) is the new ``center.'' \\\\ \\hline\n 131: \\end{tabular}\n 132: }\n 133: \n 134: \\sectionbanner{TOPIC GOALS / ESSENTIAL QUESTION / MATERIALS}\n 135: {\\small\n 136: \\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1.25}\n 137: \\noindent\\begin{tabular}{|p{1.8in}|p{4.8in}|}\n 138: \\hline\n 139: \\textbf{Topic Goals} & Students will identify asymptotes and describe translations for 6 reciprocal-function items. This is Topic 4 assessment-prep material. \\\\ \\hline\n 140: \\textbf{Essential Question} & How do the values of \\(h\\) and \\(k\\) in \\(g(x) = 1/(x-h) + k\\) determine the graph's asymptotes, domain, range, and intercepts? \\\\ \\hline\n 141: \\textbf{Materials} & Pencils; student packet; graphing calculator (optional, for verification). \\\\ \\hline\n 142: \\end{tabular}\n 143: }\n 144: \n 145: \\begin{callout}{\\IconPin\\ PRE-FLIGHT --- P3 IS ASSESSMENT-CRITICAL}{calloutpink}\n 146: Topic 4 8-Q assessment Q\\#3 (asymptotes) and Q\\#5 (translation description) both come from Ex 4 + Ex 5 content. P3 is where students earn those 2 of 8 points.\\\\\n 147: Practice \\#19 is THE rehearsal item: it has asymptotes + intercepts + implicit translation description.\\\\\n 148: No DOK-3 driver today (P2 owned the spine with \\#26). P3 is pure DOK-2 mastery.\\\\\n 149: Pace: Try-It 4 (5) \\(\\rightarrow\\) \\#11 (5) \\(\\rightarrow\\) \\#18 (6) \\(\\rightarrow\\) Try-It 5 (6) \\(\\rightarrow\\) \\#19 (8) \\(\\rightarrow\\) \\#21 (5). Total \\(\\sim\\)35 min.\\\\\n 150: If pace slows: cut \\#21 (modeling) first. Never cut \\#19.\n 151: \\end{callout}\n 152: \n 153: \\phasetag{bridge from P2}\n 154: \\frameworkphaseheader{Do Now}{1}{5}{%\n 155: \\phaseitems{\n 156:   \\item Hand out at door.\n 157:   \\item Circulate 3 min.\n 158:   \\item Cold-call for two forms (\\(y = k/x\\) and \\(xy = k\\)).\n 159: }%\n 160: }{%\n 161: \\phaseitems{\n 162:   \\item Write \\(k\\) in both forms.\n 163:   \\item Sketch prediction of \\(y = 1/x\\).\n 164: }%\n 165: }{%\n 166: \\phaseitems{\n 167:   \\item What ARE the two forms of inverse variation?\n 168:   \\item Based on yesterday: predict what \\(y = 1/x\\) looks like.\n 169: }%\n 170: }{Solo teacher: circulate silently.}\n 171: \n 172: \\bankitem{Do Now --- Generalize: \\(k\\) in terms of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\)}{%\n 173: 13. Generalize: For an inverse variation, write an equation that gives the value of \\(k\\) in terms of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).\n 174: }{}\n 175: \n 176: \\teacheranswerbox{\\IconCheck\\ ANSWER KEY}{%\n 177: \\textbf{Answer key (from TE):} \\(k = xy\\).\n 178: }\n 179: \n 180: \\begin{callout}{\\IconSearch\\ PREDICTION (spend 30 sec)}{calloutpeach}\n 181: Before we graph \\(y = 1/x\\) together, sketch what you THINK it looks like. (No wrong answers --- just predict.) We'll compare to the real thing in Launch.\n 182: \\end{callout}\n 183: \n 184: \\sectionbanner{LAUNCH --- Reciprocal Function + Translations (teacher models Ex 4 + Ex 5)}\n 185: \n 186: \\phasetag{teacher models Ex 4 + Ex 5 back-to-back}\n 187: \\frameworkphaseheader{Launch}{2}{12}{%\n 188: \\phaseitems{\n 189:   \\item FIRST 6 min: Example 4 --- graph \\(f(x) = 1/x\\). Emphasize asymptote behavior (approaching but never reaching). Show table of \\(x \\rightarrow 0\\) and \\(x \\rightarrow \\pm\\infty\\).\n 190:   \\item NEXT 5 min: Example 5 --- graph \\(g(x) = 1/(x-3) + 2\\). Identify \\((h, k) = (3, 2)\\). Asymptotes shift accordingly.\n 191:   \\item 30 sec: point at packet rule cards. ``Mark them. These are your assessment reference.''\n 192:   \\item Do NOT solve Try-Its. Release at minute 17.\n 193: }%\n 194: }{%\n 195: \\phaseitems{\n 196:   \\item Watch Ex 4. Compare your Do Now prediction to the real graph.\n 197:   \\item Watch Ex 5. Note: \\(h\\) shifts horizontal, \\(k\\) shifts vertical. \\((h, k)\\) is the new center.\n 198:   \\item Copy BOTH rule cards into margins.\n 199: }%\n 200: }{%\n 201: \\phaseitems{\n 202:   \\item Why does \\(y = 1/x\\) never equal 0? (Because \\(1/x = 0\\) has no solution.)\n 203:   \\item Why does \\(x \\neq 0\\) in the domain? (Division by zero.)\n 204:   \\item For \\(g(x) = 1/(x-3) + 2\\): what's \\(h\\)? What's \\(k\\)? Where do asymptotes cross?\n 205:   \\item What happens as \\(x \\rightarrow 3\\) from the right? From the left?\n 206: }%\n 207: }{Project both examples. Use a visible graphing tool (Desmos). Think aloud.}\n 208: \n 209: \\begin{callout}{\\IconMic\\ TEACHER SCRIPT}{calloutblue}\n 210: 1. ``Yesterday we saw \\(y = k/x\\). Today we graph it and name its features.''\\\\\n 211: 2. Example 4: ``As \\(x\\) gets huge (1000, 10000), \\(y\\) gets tiny (0.001, 0.0001) --- approaching ZERO but never reaching. That's a HORIZONTAL ASYMPTOTE at \\(y = 0\\).''\\\\\n 212: 3. ``As \\(x\\) gets close to 0, \\(y\\) explodes --- POSITIVE infinity from the right, NEGATIVE infinity from the left. That's a VERTICAL ASYMPTOTE at \\(x = 0\\).''\\\\\n 213: 4. Example 5: ``Now watch TRANSLATION. \\(g(x) = 1/(x-3) + 2\\). Subtract 3 inside \\(\\rightarrow\\) shift RIGHT 3. Add 2 outside \\(\\rightarrow\\) shift UP 2. New asymptotes: \\(x = 3\\), \\(y = 2\\).''\\\\\n 214: 5. ``The POINT \\((h, k)\\) is the center of the translated graph. For \\(g(x)\\), center is \\((3, 2)\\).''\\\\\n 215: 6. Release.\n 216: \\end{callout}\n 217: \n 218: \\begin{callout}{\\IconBook\\ RECIPROCAL FUNCTION --- PARENT \\(f(x) = 1/x\\)}{calloutgreen}\n 219: \\textbullet\\ Vertical asymptote: \\(x = 0\\)\\\\\n 220: \\textbullet\\ Horizontal asymptote: \\(y = 0\\)\\\\\n 221: \\textbullet\\ Domain: \\(\\{x \\mid x \\neq 0\\}\\) \\hspace{1.8em} \\textbullet\\ Range: \\(\\{y \\mid y \\neq 0\\}\\)\\\\\n 222: \\textbullet\\ Graph: hyperbola in Quadrants I and III (two branches, no intersection)\n 223: \\end{callout}\n 224: \n 225: \\begin{callout}{\\IconBook\\ TRANSLATED RECIPROCAL --- \\(g(x) = 1/(x-h) + k\\)}{calloutyellow}\n 226: \\textbullet\\ Vertical asymptote shifts to: \\(x = h\\)\\\\\n 227: \\textbullet\\ Horizontal asymptote shifts to: \\(y = k\\)\\\\\n 228: \\textbullet\\ Domain: \\(\\{x \\mid x \\neq h\\}\\) \\hspace{1.8em} \\textbullet\\ Range: \\(\\{y \\mid y \\neq k\\}\\)\\\\\n 229: \\textbullet\\ The point \\((h, k)\\) is the ``new center'' --- where the asymptotes cross.\\\\\n 230: \\textbullet\\ Example: \\(g(x) = 1/(x-3) + 2\\) \\(\\rightarrow\\) asymptotes \\(x = 3\\) and \\(y = 2\\).\n 231: \n 232: \\medskip\n 233: To find INTERCEPTS:\\\\\n 234: \\hspace*{1.5em}\\textbullet\\ y-intercept: set \\(x = 0\\). \\(y = 1/(0-h) + k = -1/h + k.\\)\\\\\n 235: \\hspace*{1.5em}\\textbullet\\ x-intercept: set \\(y = 0\\). Solve \\(0 = 1/(x-h) + k \\rightarrow x = h - 1/k.\\)\n 236: \\end{callout}\n 237: \n 238: \\sectionbanner{EXPLORE --- Think-Pair-Share (35 min)}\n 239: \n 240: \\phasetag{TPS --- pure DOK-2 mastery --- assessment rehearsal}\n 241: \\frameworkphaseheader{Explore}{2}{35}{%\n 242: \\phaseitems{\n 243:   \\item 6 laps across 35 min.\n 244:   \\item Laps 1-3: Try-It 4 + \\#11 + \\#18. Parent-form scaled/reflected. (5-6 min each.)\n 245:   \\item Laps 4-5: Try-It 5 + \\#19 --- translated forms. \\#19 is the assessment rehearsal --- press on BOTH asymptotes AND intercepts.\n 246:   \\item Lap 6: \\#21 --- modeling. If time.\n 247:   \\item Do not give answers. Use sentence frame to press justification.\n 248: }%\n 249: }{%\n 250: \\phaseitems{\n 251:   \\item 6 items in order. Parent forms first (Try-It 4, \\#11, \\#18), then translations (Try-It 5, \\#19, \\#21).\n 252:   \\item For \\#19: BEFORE graphing, state \\((h, k)\\), asymptotes, and domain/range.\n 253:   \\item Justify with sentence frame.\n 254: }%\n 255: }{%\n 256: \\phaseitems{\n 257:   \\item What's the center of the translation? \\((h, k)\\)\n 258:   \\item Which asymptote is which? (\\(x = h\\) vertical; \\(y = k\\) horizontal.)\n 259:   \\item For \\#11: what \\(y\\)-value does \\(y = 5/x\\) actually give at \\(x = 2\\)? (2.5 --- the labeled 4 is wrong.)\n 260:   \\item For \\#18: how does the negative flip the graph? (Quadrants II and IV instead of I and III.)\n 261:   \\item For \\#19: what are the asymptotes? What are the intercepts?\n 262: }%\n 263: }{Solo teacher: 6 laps. Press BOTH asymptotes AND intercepts on \\#19. This IS the assessment rehearsal.}\n 264: \n 265: \\textbf{Bank-item labels (in order):}\n 266: \\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em,itemsep=2pt,topsep=2pt]\n 267:   \\item Try It 4 --- Graph \\(g(x) = 10/x\\) (scaled parent)\n 268:   \\item Practice \\#11 --- Error Analysis: \\(y = 5/x\\)\n 269:   \\item Practice \\#18 --- Graph \\(y = -2/x\\) (reflected parent)\n 270:   \\item Try It 5 --- Graph \\(g(x) = 1/(x+2) - 4\\) (translated)\n 271:   \\item Practice \\#19 \\IconStar\\ ASSESSMENT CRITICAL --- \\(g(x) = 1/(x-2) + 6\\) + intercepts\n 272:   \\item Practice \\#21 --- Video game storage (modeled)\n 273: \\end{enumerate}\n 274: \n 275: \\bankitem{Try It 4 --- Graph \\(g(x) = 10/x\\) (scaled parent)}{%\n 276: Try It 4. Graph the function \\(g(x) = 10/x\\). What are the domain, range, and asymptotes of the function?\n 277: \\reciprocalblankgrid\n 278: }{}\n 279: \n 280: \\teacheranswerbox{\\IconCheck\\ ANSWER / ANTICIPATED TALK}{%\n 281: \\textbf{Answer key (from TE):} graph passes through \\((1,10)\\), \\((10,1)\\), \\((-10,-1)\\), \\((-1,-10)\\); domain: all real numbers, \\(x \\neq 0\\); range: all real numbers, \\(y \\neq 0\\); horizontal asymptote \\(y = 0\\); vertical asymptote \\(x = 0\\). Graph of \\(g(x) = 10/x\\) is a vertical stretch of \\(f(x) = 1/x\\).\n 282: }\n 283: \n 284: \\bankitem{Practice \\#11 --- Error Analysis: \\(y = 5/x\\)}{%\n 285: 11. Error Analysis: Describe and correct the error a student made in graphing the function \\(y = 5/x\\). The student's graph shows labeled points \\((1, 5)\\), \\((2, 4)\\), \\((5, 1)\\) in Quadrant I and \\((-5, -1)\\), \\((-2, -4)\\), \\((-1, -5)\\) in Quadrant III, with a large red X indicating the graph is incorrect.\n 286: \\qelevengraph\n 287: }{}\n 288: \n 289: \\teacheranswerbox{\\IconCheck\\ ANSWER / ANTICIPATED TALK}{%\n 290: \\textbf{Answer key (from TE):} The points \\((2, 4)\\) and \\((-2, -4)\\) are not correct. The labels of these points should be \\((2, 2.5)\\) and \\((-2, -2.5)\\). Reason: \\(y = 5/2 = 2.5\\), not 4.\n 291: }\n 292: \n 293: \\bankitem{Practice \\#18 --- Graph \\(y = -2/x\\) (reflected parent)}{%\n 294: 18. Graph the function \\(y = -2/x\\). What are the domain, range, and asymptotes of the function?\n 295: \\reciprocalblankgrid\n 296: }{}\n 297: \n 298: \\teacheranswerbox{\\IconCheck\\ ANSWER / ANTICIPATED TALK}{%\n 299: \\textbf{Answer key (from TE):} vertical asymptote: \\(x = 0\\); horizontal asymptote: \\(y = 0\\); domain: the set of real numbers except 0 (\\(x \\neq 0\\)); range: the set of real numbers except 0 (\\(y \\neq 0\\)). The graph is the reciprocal function reflected over the \\(x\\)-axis (Quadrants II and IV) and vertically stretched by a factor of 2.\n 300: }\n 301: \n 302: \\bankitem{Try It 5 --- Graph \\(g(x) = 1/(x+2) - 4\\) (translated)}{%\n 303: Try It 5. Graph \\(g(x) = 1/(x + 2) - 4\\). What are the equations of the asymptotes? What are the domain and range?\n 304: \\reciprocalblankgrid\n 305: }{}\n 306: \n 307: \\teacheranswerbox{\\IconCheck\\ ANSWER / ANTICIPATED TALK}{%\n 308: \\textbf{Answer key (from TE):} vertical asymptote \\(x = -2\\); horizontal asymptote \\(y = -4\\); domain: set of real numbers with \\(x \\neq -2\\); range: set of real numbers with \\(y \\neq -4\\). Graph translated 2 units left and 4 units down from parent.\n 309: }\n 310: \n 311: \\bankitem{Practice \\#19 \\IconStar\\ ASSESSMENT CRITICAL --- \\(g(x) = 1/(x-2) + 6\\) + intercepts}{%\n 312: 19. Graph \\(g(x) = 1/(x - 2) + 6\\). What are the equations of the asymptotes? What are the domain and range? What are the intercepts?\n 313: \\reciprocalblankgrid\n 314: }{}\n 315: \n 316: \\teacheranswerbox{\\IconCheck\\ ANSWER / ANTICIPATED TALK}{%\n 317: \\textbf{Answer key (from TE):} vertical asymptote \\(x = 2\\); horizontal asymptote \\(y = 6\\); domain: set of real numbers with \\(x \\neq 2\\); range: set of real numbers with \\(y \\neq 6\\); \\(y\\)-intercept: \\(5.5\\) (or \\(11/2\\)); \\(x\\)-intercept: \\(11/6\\). Graph shows translated reciprocal function with asymptotes intersecting at \\((2, 6)\\).\n 318: }\n 319: \n 320: \\bankitem{Practice \\#21 --- Video game storage (modeled)}{%\n 321: 21. Use Structure: The number of downloaded games that can be stored on a video game system varies inversely with the average size of a video game. A certain video game system can store 160 games when the average size of a game is 2.0 gigabytes (GB). (a) Write an inverse equation that relates the number of games \\(n\\) that will fit on the video game system as a function of the average game size \\(s\\) in GB. (b) Use the inverse relationship to complete the table of values. Game Size (GB), \\(s\\): 1.0, 2.5, 3.0, 4.0; Number of Games, \\(n\\): \\_, \\_, \\_, \\_. (c) Sketch a graph of this inverse relationship on a coordinate plane.\n 322: \\begin{center}\n 323: \\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1.35}\n 324: \\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|c|c|}\n 325: \\hline\n 326: \\textbf{Game Size (GB), \\(s\\)} & 1.0 & 2.5 & 3.0 & 4.0 \\\\ \\hline\n 327: \\textbf{Number of Games, \\(n\\)} & \\entrybox & \\entrybox & \\entrybox & \\entrybox \\\\ \\hline\n 328: \\end{tabular}\n 329: \\end{center}\n 330: \\gamesblankgrid\n 331: }{}\n 332: \n 333: \\teacheranswerbox{\\IconCheck\\ ANSWER / ANTICIPATED TALK}{%\n 334: \\textbf{Answer key (from TE):} (a) \\(n = 320/s\\) (since \\(k = 160 \\cdot 2.0 = 320\\)). (b) Table values: \\(s = 1.0 \\rightarrow n = 320\\); \\(s = 2.5 \\rightarrow n = 128\\); \\(s = 3.0 \\rightarrow n = 106.7\\); \\(s = 4.0 \\rightarrow n = 80\\). (c) Hyperbola through labeled points \\((1, 320)\\), \\((2, 160)\\) in Quadrant I and \\((-1, -320)\\), \\((-2, -160)\\) in Quadrant III (though physical context only supports Quadrant I).\n 335: }\n 336: \n 337: \\sectionbanner{SHARE / SUMMARY (5 min)}\n 338: \n 339: \\phasetag{Concept Summary + LEHS 8-Q preview}\n 340: \\frameworkphaseheader{Share / Summary}{2}{5}{%\n 341: \\phaseitems{\n 342:   \\item Cold-call 1 pair to present \\#19 (asymptotes + intercepts).\n 343:   \\item Project Concept Summary (from student packet).\n 344:   \\item ``Topic 4 8-Q assessment: expect 2 of 8 questions on THIS content --- asymptotes and translation descriptions. You just rehearsed both.''\n 345: }%\n 346: }{%\n 347: \\phaseitems{\n 348:   \\item Presenting pair uses sentence frame for \\#19.\n 349:   \\item Rest of class signals and annotates their Concept Summary.\n 350: }%\n 351: }{%\n 352: \\phaseitems{\n 353:   \\item What are the TWO big ideas from Lesson 4-1?\n 354:   \\item If you see \\(g(x) = a/(x-h) + k\\) on the assessment, what's the FIRST thing you identify?\n 355: }%\n 356: }{Frame today as assessment rehearsal. Students leave confident.}\n 357: \n 358: \\begin{sentenceframebox}\n 359: \\textbf{Sentence frames}\n 360: \n 361: Pair share (\\#19): ``This graph is the parent \\(f(x) = 1/x\\) translated \\_\\_\\_ units \\_\\_\\_ and \\_\\_\\_ units \\_\\_\\_. The asymptotes are \\(x =\\) \\_\\_\\_ and \\(y =\\) \\_\\_\\_. The intercepts are \\_\\_\\_.''\n 362: \n 363: Whole class: one pair reads \\#19. Class signals thumbs up/sideways.\n 364: \\end{sentenceframebox}\n 365: \n 366: \\begin{callout}{\\IconChart\\ CONCEPT SUMMARY (your reference for Topic 4 assessment)}{calloutell}\n 367: INVERSE VARIATION (\\(y = k/x\\)):\\\\\n 368: \\hspace*{1.5em}--- WORDS: As one variable increases, the other decreases; their product \\(k\\) is constant.\\\\\n 369: \\hspace*{1.5em}--- ALGEBRA: \\(y = k/x\\) where \\(k \\neq 0\\)\\\\\n 370: \\hspace*{1.5em}--- GRAPH: parent hyperbola, asymptotes \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y = 0\\)\n 371: \n 372: \\medskip\n 373: RECIPROCAL FUNCTION TRANSLATIONS (\\(y = a/(x-h) + k\\)):\\\\\n 374: \\hspace*{1.5em}--- WORDS: The reciprocal function can shift left/right (\\(h\\)) and up/down (\\(k\\)).\\\\\n 375: \\hspace*{1.5em}--- ALGEBRA: \\(y = a/(x-h) + k\\)\\\\\n 376: \\hspace*{1.5em}--- GRAPH: asymptotes shift to \\(x = h\\) and \\(y = k\\); new ``center'' at \\((h, k)\\).\n 377: \\end{callout}\n 378: \n 379: \\sectionbanner{EXIT TICKET}\n 380: \n 381: \\phasetag{pre-assessment confidence check}\n 382: \\frameworkphaseheader{Exit Ticket}{1-2}{3}{%\n 383: \\phaseitems{\n 384:   \\item Collect at door.\n 385:   \\item Tonight: scan confidence scores. If averages <3 on asymptotes or translations, insert a 5-min Do Now recap when the 8-Q lands.\n 386: }%\n 387: }{%\n 388: \\phaseitems{\n 389:   \\item Rate