els Ex 4 + Ex 5)}\n 185: \n 186: \\phasetag{teacher models Ex 4 + Ex 5 back-to-back}\n 187: \\frameworkphaseheader{Launch}{2}{12}{%\n 188: \\phaseitems{\n 189:   \\item FIRST 6 min: Example 4 --- graph \\(f(x) = 1/x\\). Emphasize asymptote behavior (approaching but never reaching). Show table of \\(x \\rightarrow 0\\) and \\(x \\rightarrow \\pm\\infty\\).\n 190:   \\item NEXT 5 min: Example 5 --- graph \\(g(x) = 1/(x-3) + 2\\). Identify \\((h, k) = (3, 2)\\). Asymptotes shift accordingly.\n 191:   \\item 30 sec: point at packet rule cards. ``Mark them. These are your assessment reference.''\n 192:   \\item Do NOT solve Try-Its. Release at minute 17.\n 193: }%\n 194: }{%\n 195: \\phaseitems{\n 196:   \\item Watch Ex 4. Compare your Do Now prediction to the real graph.\n 197:   \\item Watch Ex 5. Note: \\(h\\) shifts horizontal, \\(k\\) shifts vertical. \\((h, k)\\) is the new center.\n 198:   \\item Copy BOTH rule cards into margins.\n 199: }%\n 200: }{%\n 201: \\phaseitems{\n 202:   \\item Why does \\(y = 1/x\\) never equal 0? (Because \\(1/x = 0\\) has no solution.)\n 203:   \\item Why does \\(x \\neq 0\\) in the domain? (Division by zero.)\n 204:   \\item For \\(g(x) = 1/(x-3) + 2\\): what's \\(h\\)? What's \\(k\\)? Where do asymptotes cross?\n 205:   \\item What happens as \\(x \\rightarrow 3\\) from the right? From the left?\n 206: }%\n 207: }{Project both examples. Use a visible graphing tool (Desmos). Think aloud.}\n 208: \n 209: \\begin{callout}{\\IconMic\\ TEACHER SCRIPT}{calloutblue}\n 210: 1. ``Yesterday we saw \\(y = k/x\\). Today we graph it and name its features.''\\\\\n 211: 2. Example 4: ``As \\(x\\) gets huge (1000, 10000), \\(y\\) gets tiny (0.001, 0.0001) --- approaching ZERO but never reaching. That's a HORIZONTAL ASYMPTOTE at \\(y = 0\\).''\\\\\n 212: 3. ``As \\(x\\) gets close to 0, \\(y\\) explodes --- POSITIVE infinity from the right, NEGATIVE infinity from the left. That's a VERTICAL ASYMPTOTE at \\(x = 0\\).''\\\\\n 213: 4. Example 5: ``Now watch TRANSLATION. \\(g(x) = 1/(x-3) + 2\\). Subtract 3 inside \\(\\rightarrow\\) shift RIGHT 3. Add 2 outside \\(\\rightarrow\\) shift UP 2. New asymptotes: \\(x = 3\\), \\(y = 2\\).''\\\\\n 214: 5. ``The POINT \\((h, k)\\) is the center of the translated graph. For \\(g(x)\\), center is \\((3, 2)\\).''\\\\\n 215: 6. Release.\n 216: \\end{callout}\n 217: \n 218: \\begin{callout}{\\IconBook\\ RECIPROCAL FUNCTION --- PARENT \\(f(x) = 1/x\\)}{calloutgreen}\n 219: \\textbullet\\ Vertical asymptote: \\(x = 0\\)\\\\\n 220: \\textbullet\\ Horizontal asymptote: \\(y = 0\\)\\\\\n 221: \\textbullet\\ Domain: \\(\\{x \\mid x \\neq 0\\}\\) \\hspace{1.8em} \\textbullet\\ Range: \\(\\{y \\mid y \\neq 0\\}\\)\\\\\n 222: \\textbullet\\ Graph: hyperbola in Quadrants I and III (two branches, no intersection)\n 223: \\end{callout}\n 224: \n 225: \\begin{callout}{\\IconBook\\ TRANSLATED RECIPROCAL --- \\(g(x) = 1/(x-h) + k\\)}{calloutyellow}\n 226: \\textbullet\\ Vertical asymptote shifts to: \\(x = h\\)\\\\\n 227: \\textbullet\\ Horizontal asymptote shifts to: \\(y = k\\)\\\\\n 228: \\textbullet\\ Domain: \\(\\{x \\mid x \\neq h\\}\\) \\hspace{1.8em} \\textbullet\\ Range: \\(\\{y \\mid y \\neq k\\}\\)\\\\\n 229: \\textbullet\\ The point \\((h, k)\\) is the ``new center'' --- where the asymptotes cross.\\\\\n 230: \\textbullet\\ Example: \\(g(x) = 1/(x-3) + 2\\) \\(\\rightarrow\\) asymptotes \\(x = 3\\) and \\(y = 2\\).\n 231: \n 232: \\medskip\n 233: To find INTERCEPTS:\\\\\n 234: \\hspace*{1.5em}\\textbullet\\ y-intercept: set \\(x = 0\\). \\(y = 1/(0-h) + k = -1/h + k.\\)\\\\\n 235: \\hspace*{1.5em}\\textbullet\\ x-intercept: set \\(y = 0\\). Solve \\(0 = 1/(x-h) + k \\rightarrow x = h - 1/k.\\)\n 236: \\end{callout}\n 237: \n 238: \\sectionbanner{EXPLORE --- Think-Pair-Share (35 min)}\n 239: \n 240: \\phasetag{TPS --- pure DOK-2 mastery --- assessment rehearsal}\n 241: \\frameworkphaseheader{Explore}{2}{35}{%\n 242: \\phaseitems{\n 243:   \\item 6 laps across 35 min.\n 244:   \\item Laps 1-3: Try-It 4 + \\#11 + \\#18. Parent-form scaled/reflected. (5-6 min each.)\n 245:   \\item Laps 4-5: Try-It 5 + \\#19 --- translated forms. \\#19 is the assessment rehearsal --- press on BOTH asymptotes AND intercepts.\n 246:   \\item Lap 6: \\#21 --- modeling. If time.\n 247:   \\item Do not give answers. Use sentence frame to press justification.\n 248: }%\n 249: }{%\n 250: \\phaseitems{\n 251:   \\item 6 items in order. Parent forms first (Try-It 4, \\#11, \\#18), then translations (Try-It 5, \\#19, \\#21).\n 252:   \\item For \\#19: BEFORE graphing, state \\((h, k)\\), asymptotes, and domain/range.\n 253:   \\item Justify with sentence frame.\n 254: }%\n 255: }{%\n 256: \\phaseitems{\n 257:   \\item What's the center of the translation? \\((h, k)\\)\n 258:   \\item Which asymptote is which? (\\(x = h\\) vertical; \\(y = k\\) horizontal.)\n 259:   \\item For \\#11: what \\(y\\)-value does \\(y = 5/x\\) actually give at \\(x = 2\\)? (2.5 --- the labeled 4 is wrong.)\n 260:   \\item For \\#18: how does the negative flip the graph? (Quadrants II and IV instead of I and III.)\n 261:   \\item For \\#19: what are the asymptotes? What are the intercepts?\n 262: }%\n 263: }{Solo teacher: 6 laps. Press BOTH asymptotes AND intercepts on \\#19. This IS the assessment rehearsal.}\n 264: \n 265: \\textbf{Bank-item labels (in order):}\n 266: \\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em,itemsep=2pt,topsep=2pt]\n 267:   \\item Try It 4 --- Graph \\(g(x) = 10/x\\) (scaled parent)\n 268:   \\item Practice \\#11 --- Error Analysis: \\(y = 5/x\\)\n 269:   \\item Practice \\#18 --- Graph \\(y = -2/x\\) (reflected parent)\n 270:   \\item Try It 5 --- Graph \\(g(x) = 1/(x+2) - 4\\) (translated)\n 271:   \\item Practice \\#19 \\IconStar\\ ASSESSMENT CRITICAL --- \\(g(x) = 1/(x-2) + 6\\) + intercepts\n 272:   \\item Practice \\#21 --- Video game storage (modeled)\n 273: \\end{enumerate}\n 274: \n 275: \\bankitem{Try It 4 --- Graph \\(g(x) = 10/x\\) (scaled parent)}{%\n 276: Try It 4. Graph the function \\(g(x) = 10/x\\). What are the domain, range, and asymptotes of the function?\n 277: \\reciprocalblankgrid\n 278: }{}\n 279: \n 280: \\teacheranswerbox{\\IconCheck\\ ANSWER / ANTICIPATED TALK}{%\n 281: \\textbf{Answer key (from TE):} graph passes through \\((1,10)\\), \\((10,1)\\), \\((-10,-1)\\), \\((-1,-10)\\); domain: all real numbers, \\(x \\neq 0\\); range: all real numbers, \\(y \\neq 0\\); horizontal asymptote \\(y = 0\\); vertical asymptote \\(x = 0\\). Graph of \\(g(x) = 10/x\\) is a vertical stretch of \\(f(x) = 1/x\\).\n 282: }\n 283: \n 284: \\bankitem{Practice \\#11 --- Error Analysis: \\(y = 5/x\\)}{%\n 285: 11. Error Analysis: Describe and correct the error a student made in graphing the function \\(y = 5/x\\). The student's graph shows labeled points \\((1, 5)\\), \\((2, 4)\\), \\((5, 1)\\) in Quadrant I and \\((-5, -1)\\), \\((-2, -4)\\), \\((-1, -5)\\) in Quadrant III, with a large red X indicating the graph is incorrect.\n 286: \\qelevengraph\n 287: }{}\n 288: \n 289: \\teacheranswerbox{\\IconCheck\\ ANSWER / ANTICIPATED TALK}{%\n 290: \\textbf{Answer key (from TE):} The points \\((2, 4)\\) and \\((-2, -4)\\) are not correct. The labels of these points should be \\((2, 2.5)\\) and \\((-2, -2.5)\\). Reason: \\(y = 5/2 = 2.5\\), not 4.\n 291: }\n 292: \n 293: \\bankitem{Practice \\#18 --- Graph \\(y = -2/x\\) (reflected parent)}{%\n 294: 18. Graph the function \\(y = -2/x\\). What are the domain, range, and asymptotes of the function?\n 295: \\reciprocalblankgrid\n 296: }{}\n 297: \n 298: \\teacheranswerbox{\\IconCheck\\ ANSWER / ANTICIPATED TALK}{%\n 299: \\textbf{Answer key (from TE):} vertical asymptote: \\(x = 0\\); horizontal asymptote: \\(y = 0\\); domain: the set of real numbers except 0 (\\(x \\neq 0\\)); range: the set of real numbers except 0 (\\(y \\neq 0\\)). The graph is the reciprocal function reflected over the \\(x\\)-axis (Quadrants II and IV) and vertically stretched by a factor of 2.\n 300: }\n 301: \n 302: \\bankitem{Try It 5 --- Graph \\(g(x) = 1/(x+2) - 4\\) (translated)}{%\n 303: Try It 5. Graph \\(g(x) = 1/(x + 2) - 4\\). What are the equations of the asymptotes? What are the domain and range?\n 304: \\reciprocalblankgrid\n 305: }{}\n 306: \n 307: \\teacheranswerbox{\\IconCheck\\ ANSWER / ANTICIPATED TALK}{%\n 308: \\textbf{Answer key (from TE):} vertical asymptote \\(x = -2\\); horizontal asymptote \\(y = -4\\); domain: set of real numbers with \\(x \\neq -2\\); range: set of real numbers with \\(y \\neq -4\\). Graph translated 2 units left and 4 units down from parent.\n 309: }\n 310: \n 311: \\bankitem{Practice \\#19 \\IconStar\\ ASSESSMENT CRITICAL --- \\(g(x) = 1/(x-2) + 6\\) + intercepts}{%\n 312: 19. Graph \\(g(x) = 1/(x - 2) + 6\\). What are the equations of the asymptotes? What are the domain and range? What are the intercepts?\n 313: \\reciprocalblankgrid\n 314: }{}\n 315: \n 316: \\teacheranswerbox{\\IconCheck\\ ANSWER / ANTICIPATED TALK}{%\n 317: \\textbf{Answer key (from TE):} vertical asymptote \\(x = 2\\); horizontal asymptote \\(y = 6\\); domain: set of real numbers with \\(x \\neq 2\\); range: set of real numbers with \\(y \\neq 6\\); \\(y\\)-intercept: \\(5.5\\) (or \\(11/2\\)); \\(x\\)-intercept: \\(11/6\\). Graph shows translated reciprocal function with asymptotes intersecting at \\((2, 6)\\).\n 318: }\n 319: \n 320: \\bankitem{Practice \\#21 --- Video game storage (modeled)}{%\n 321: 21. Use Structure: The number of downloaded games that can be stored on a video game system varies inversely with the average size of a video game. A certain video game system can store 160 games when the average size of a game is 2.0 gigabytes (GB). (a) Write an inverse equation that relates the number of games \\(n\\) that will fit on the video game system as a function of the average game size \\(s\\) in GB. (b) Use the inverse relationship to complete the table of values. Game Size (GB), \\(s\\): 1.0, 2.5, 3.0, 4.0; Number of Games, \\(n\\): \\_, \\_, \\_, \\_. (c) Sketch a graph of this inverse relationship on a coordinate plane.\n 322: \\begin{center}\n 323: \\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1.35}\n 324: \\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|c|c|}\n 325: \\hline\n 326: \\textbf{Game Size (GB), \\(s\\)} & 1.0 & 2.5 & 3.0 & 4.0 \\\\ \\hline\n 327: \\textbf{Number of Games, \\(n\\)} & \\entrybox & \\entrybox & \\entrybox & \\entrybox \\\\ \\hline\n 328: \\end{tabular}\n 329: \\end{center}\n 330: \\gamesblankgrid\n 331: }{}\n 332: \n 333: \\teacheranswerbox{\\IconCheck\\ ANSWER / ANTICIPATED TALK}{%\n 334: \\textbf{Answer key (from TE):} (a) \\(n = 320/s\\) (since \\(k = 160 \\cdot 2.0 = 320\\)). (b) Table values: \\(s = 1.0 \\rightarrow n = 320\\); \\(s = 2.5 \\rightarrow n = 128\\); \\(s = 3.0 \\rightarrow n = 106.7\\); \\(s = 4.0 \\rightarrow n = 80\\). (c) Hyperbola through labeled points \\((1, 320)\\), \\((2, 160)\\) in Quadrant I and \\((-1, -320)\\), \\((-2, -160)\\) in Quadrant III (though physical context only supports Quadrant I).\n 335: }\n 336: \n 337: \\sectionbanner{SHARE / SUMMARY (5 min)}\n 338: \n 339: \\phasetag{Concept Summary + LEHS 8-Q preview}\n 340: \\frameworkphaseheader{Share / Summary}{2}{5}{%\n 341: \\phaseitems{\n 342:   \\item Cold-call 1 pair to present \\#19 (asymptotes + intercepts).\n 343:   \\item Project Concept Summary (from student packet).\n 344:   \\item ``Topic 4 8-Q assessment: expect 2 of 8 questions on THIS content --- asymptotes and translation descriptions. You just rehearsed both.''\n 345: }%\n 346: }{%\n 347: \\phaseitems{\n 348:   \\item Presenting pair uses sentence frame for \\#19.\n 349:   \\item Rest of class signals and annotates their Concept Summary.\n 350: }%\n 351: }{%\n 352: \\phaseitems{\n 353:   \\item What are the TWO big ideas from Lesson 4-1?\n 354:   \\item If you see \\(g(x) = a/(x-h) + k\\) on the assessment, what's the FIRST thing you identify?\n 355: }%\n 356: }{Frame today as assessment rehearsal. Students leave confident.}\n 357: \n 358: \\begin{sentenceframebox}\n 359: \\t