? \\\\ \\hline\n  40: \\textbf{Materials} & Pencils; student packet; calculator (optional). No Chromebooks required for Period 1. \\\\ \\hline\n  41: \\end{tabular}\n  42: }\n  43: \n  44: \\sectionbanner{LAUNCH \u00e2\u20ac\u201d Inverse Variation (teacher models on screen)}\n  45: \n  46: \\begin{callout}{\\IconBook\\ INVERSE VARIATION RULES (keep printed on your page)}{calloutgreen}\n  47: \\textbullet\\ Two variables vary INVERSELY when their product is a constant \\(k\\):\\\\\n  48: \\hspace*{1.6em}\\(xy = k\\) \\hspace{1.5em} or equivalently \\hspace{1.5em} \\(y = k/x\\)\\\\\n  49: \\textbullet\\ The constant \\(k\\) is the CONSTANT OF VARIATION.\\\\\n  50: \\textbullet\\ As one variable INCREASES, the other DECREASES proportionally.\\\\\n  51: \\textbullet\\ Example: if \\(x = 10\\) and \\(y = 3\\) vary inversely, then \\(k = xy = 30\\).\\\\\n  52: \\hspace*{1.6em}Equation: \\(y = 30/x\\). When \\(x = -6\\): \\(y = 30/(-6) = -5\\).\n  53: \\end{callout}\n  54: \n  55: \\begin{callout}{\\IconWarn\\ COMMON ERROR}{calloutred}\n  56: Do NOT write \\(y/x = k\\) \u00e2\u20ac\u201d that is DIRECT variation.\\\\\n  57: Inverse variation multiplies, direct variation divides.\\\\\n  58: Test: compute \\(xy\\) for several points. If constant \\(\\rightarrow\\) inverse.\n  59: \\end{callout}\n  60: \n  61: \\sectionbanner{EXPLORE \u00e2\u20ac\u201d Think-Pair-Share (33 min)}\n  62: \\textit{Work with a partner. Use the Inverse Variation Rules above for every problem. Move through items in order \u00e2\u20ac\u201d each builds on the last.}\n  63: \n  64: \\textbf{Bank-item labels (in order):}\n  65: \\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em,itemsep=2pt,topsep=2pt]\n  66:   \\item Try It 1 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d Identify inverse variation\n  67:   \\item Practice \\#9 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d Generalize\n  68:   \\item Practice \\#14 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d Another table check\n  69:   \\item Try It 2 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d Write the equation\n  70:   \\item Practice \\#13 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d Gener