eralize: For an inverse variation, write an equation that gives the value of \\(k\\) in terms of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).\n 104: }{}\n 105: \n 106: \\bankitem{Practice \\#16 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d Apply the equation}{%\n 107: 16. If \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) vary inversely and \\(x = 3\\) when \\(y = \\frac{2}{3}\\), what is the value of \\(y\\) when \\(x = -1\\)?\n 108: }{}\n 109: \n 110: \\bankitem{Practice \\#10 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d Construct Arguments (extension)}{%\n 111: 10. Construct Arguments: Explain why zero cannot be in the domain of an inverse variation.\n 112: }{}\n 113: \n 114: \\sectionbanner{SHARE / SUMMARY (5 min)}\n 115: \n 116: \\begin{sentenceframebox}\n 117: \\textbf{Sentence frame}\n 118: \n 119: Pair share: \u00e2\u20ac\u0153As \\_\\_\\_ increases, \\_\\_\\_ decreases. Their product is \\_\\_\\_, so the equation is \\(y = \\_\\_\\_/x\\).\u00e2\u20ac\u009d\n 120: \n 121: Whole class: one pair reads their sentence. Class signals thumbs up/sideways.\n 122: \\end{sentenceframebox}\n 123: \n 124: \\sectionbanner{EXIT}\n 125: \n 126: \\begin{summaryexitbox}{EXIT TICKET \u00e2\u20ac\u201d Summary Recap}\n 127: Today I learned that \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_.\n 128: \n 129: The difference between inverse and direct variation is \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_.\n 130: \n 131: One thing I'm still unsure about is \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_.\n 132: \\end{summaryexitbox}\n 133: \n 134: \\clearpage\n 135: \n 136: \\sectionbanner{OPTIONAL REINFORCEMENT (not collected)}\n 137: \\textit{If you finish Explore early or want extra practice before Period 2.}\n 138: \n 139: \\begin{callout}{\\IconScope\\ OPTIONAL \u00c2\u00b7 Model \\& Di